Welcome to notebook five. The previous notebook gave you the data shapes. List, Vector, Set, Map, and Array. Notebook three gave you function values. This notebook puts the two together, and introduces the higher order operations that take a collection and a function and return a new collection.

Here is the unifying idea for this whole session. Almost every Scala collection supports the same operation vocabulary. List has map, filter, flatMap, fold, groupBy, and zip. Vector has them. Set has them. Map has them. Even Array has them. Learn the vocabulary once, and you can apply it everywhere. Even better, Spark's data set and data frame application programming interfaces are the same operations, lifted to a distributed setting. The map you call on a local List is conceptually the same map Spark runs across a cluster.

Let us start with the most common operation. Map. The method map applies a function to every element of a collection and returns a new collection of the same shape, filled with the results. The shape of the output is determined by two things. The output element type comes from the function's return type. If you map an Int collection with a function that returns String, the output is a String collection. The output collection type stays the same as the input. List dot map returns a List. Vector dot map returns a Vector. Set dot map returns a Set.

Three things to register about map. First, the output collection has the same length as the input. Second, the element type changes if the function changes it. Third, the collection type stays. Those three rules cover almost every use of map.

Next is filter. Filter takes a predicate, which is a function returning Boolean, and returns a new collection containing only the elements where the predicate is true. There is also filterNot, which is the inverse. It keeps only the elements where the predicate is false.

Four close cousins of filter are worth knowing. Exists takes a predicate and returns true if at least one element matches. ForAll takes a predicate and returns true if every element matches. Count takes a predicate and returns the number of matching elements. Find takes a predicate and returns the first matching element wrapped in an Option. None if nothing matches. These four are quick wins. When you find yourself writing filter followed by a check on the result, one of these four probably does it in one call.

Map and filter together cover the vast majority of everyday transformations. The next operation lets you do things they cannot.

Flat map. Flat map is map followed by flatten. The function you give it returns a collection per input element, and flat map concatenates those collections into one. Where map gives you a collection of collections, flat map gives you a single flat collection. For example, if you have a list of two strings, each containing several words, mapping each string to its word split produces a list of lists. Flat mapping with the same function produces a single list of all the words. Flat map shows up whenever you have a one to many transformation. Each input element produces zero or more output elements, and you want a single flat collection at the end. This same shape generalises beyond collections. In notebook nine, flat map on Option is how you chain operations that might fail. In notebook thirteen, flat map on Future is how you chain asynchronous computations. The pattern is the same. Bind a value, do something with it that might produce more values, get them flattened together.

Now we move from operations that produce new collections to operations that collapse a collection to a single value. Reduce and fold. Reduce takes a binary combining function and applies it across the collection. For a list of numbers, reduce of the addition operator gives the sum. Reduce of the max operator gives the maximum. Reduce has one important constraint though. It needs at least one element. Calling reduce on an empty collection throws an exception, because reduce has no starting value to return.

Fold solves that problem. The method foldLeft takes an explicit starting value, which functional programmers call the zero, and a combining function. It works on empty collections, because if the collection is empty, foldLeft just returns the zero. The signature of foldLeft is unusual. It uses two parameter lists. The first list holds the zero. The second list holds the combining function. The function takes the running accumulator and the next element, and returns the next accumulator. Why two parameter lists? Because the compiler infers the accumulator's type from the zero in the first list, and that inferred type is then known when it reads the second list. You almost never have to write the accumulator's type explicitly.

For common folds, Scala ships shortcuts. The method sum adds all the numeric elements. Product multiplies them. Min finds the minimum. Max finds the maximum. Mkstring concatenates string representations of all elements, optionally with a separator. Reach for these shortcuts first. Use foldLeft for the cases the shortcuts do not cover, like building up a non standard accumulator or combining elements in a way that no shortcut captures.

Next come the grouping operations. The method groupBy takes a key function and returns a Map from key to list of elements bucketed by what the key function returned. For example, group a list of words by their first letter, and you get a Map from each letter to the list of words starting with that letter. This is exactly what Spark's groupBy is built on, lifted to a distributed setting. The local in memory version is the same shape. Pick a key function, get a Map of key to elements.

The method partition is the Boolean version of groupBy. It takes a predicate and splits the collection into two parts. The elements that match and the elements that do not. The return value is a tuple of two collections. You typically destructure that tuple on the left side of a val, binding both halves at once. So you can write, val open paren evens comma odds close paren equals nums dot partition of the even predicate. That single line gives you two named lists.

Combining two collections side by side is what zip is for. Zip takes another collection and pairs elements position by position, stopping at the shorter of the two. So if you have a list of names and a list of ages, zipping them gives you a list of name and age tuples. There is a useful variant called zipWithIndex, which pairs each element with its position in the collection. That is the every day trick. Pair each element with its index so you can use both inside a map or a filter.

Sorting comes in three flavours, depending on what feels natural at the call site. The method sorted, with no argument, uses the natural ordering for types that have one. Int, Long, Double, String, all sort the way you would expect. For types that do not have a natural ordering, you need to provide one. Notebook eleven explains how, using givens. The method sortBy takes a function from element to key, and sorts by the key. That is the every day choice. You write a small function that extracts the sorting field, and sortBy does the rest. The method sortWith takes an explicit comparison function that returns true if the first argument should come before the second. Reach for sortWith when the comparison is more complex than a single key.

Now we arrive at one of the most important syntactic constructs in Scala. The for comprehension. The for comprehension is pure syntactic sugar that desugars into a chain of map, flat map, and withFilter. Once you know that, the syntax stops feeling like magic, and you can read any for comprehension by mechanically translating it back to the chain of method calls.

The simplest case is one generator, no filter. The expression, for n taking values from a list, yield, the square of n, desugars directly to the list dot map of the function from n to the square of n. They produce the same result. They are the same code.

If you add an if guard, the for comprehension desugars to withFilter followed by map. The guard becomes the withFilter, and the yield expression becomes the map. So a for comprehension that pulls n from a list, keeps only the even ones, and yields the square, desugars to list dot withFilter of the even predicate, dot map of the square function.

If you add a second generator inside the for, the outer generator becomes a flat map and the inner one becomes a map. So a for comprehension that pulls x from one list and y from another list, yielding the tuple of x and y, desugars to the outer list dot flatMap of, x arrow, the inner list dot map of, y arrow, the tuple. That is the cartesian product, with two lines of for, instead of two nested lambdas.

The complete desugaring rule is just four lines. The last generator together with the yield becomes a map. Every earlier generator becomes a flat map on the previous result. An if guard becomes a withFilter. And if you write for without yield, just for and do, it becomes foreach, which runs the body for its side effect. That is the entire for comprehension specification.

Everything else is shape and indentation. The same syntax works for any type that defines map, flat map, and with filter. Including Option, Either, Future, and Spark's data set. Notebook nine will use for over Option chains. Notebook thirteen will use it over Future chains. The for syntax is the unifying surface for any container that supports those three methods.

One last topic before we close. Views. Every operation you have seen so far is strict. It produces a new collection right away. If you chain three maps, you allocate three intermediate collections. For long pipelines on big inputs, that is wasteful. The fix is the view. A view is a lazy projection of a collection. Operations on a view are recorded but not executed until you ask for the result. You enter laziness by calling dot view, and you exit by calling dot toList, or dot toVector, or dot toMap, which forces the pipeline to actually run.

Views shine in two cases. Long pipelines where the intermediate collections would be large. And early exit operations like take of n, find, and exists, where you do not need to process the full input. In those cases, the view stops as soon as it has enough results. When should you not use views? On short pipelines and small data. The lazy machinery itself has overhead. For a ten element list with two transformations, strict evaluation is both faster and simpler to read.

One important note. Spark's resilient distributed data sets and data frames are always lazy. Every transformation in Spark is a recipe. Nothing runs until an action like collect or count is called. You will feel the same pattern that views give you locally, just at cluster scale. The vocabulary you build here on Vector and List is the vocabulary you will reuse on Spark data sets in notebook fifteen.

To close, here is a small pipeline that uses several of the operations together. The goal is to compute total revenue per customer from a list of orders. Each order has a customer name, an item name, and an amount. You group the orders by customer, which produces a Map from customer to a list of their orders. You enter a view to avoid intermediate allocation. You map the values, replacing each list of orders with the sum of the order amounts. You force the result back to a Map. The pipeline reads top to bottom in the order it conceptually happens. Group, sum, materialise. Compare that to a hand written var and loop version. Half the lines. No mutation. The shape of the logic is visible at a glance. That readability is the whole point of the vocabulary.

Two notebooks finished the data half of Scala. Shapes in notebook four. Operations in notebook five. The next two notebooks pivot to modeling. Notebook six introduces classes, objects, and traits. The tools Scala uses to package behaviour into reusable types. Notebook seven then introduces case classes and enums, which are the form most domain modeling in Scala takes.
